    <section>
      <h2>Roundtable discussions</h2>
      <p>Beyond lectures, ICPS 2028 aims to create spaces where physics can engage with the broader challenges facing contemporary society.</p>
      <p>The Organizing Committee therefore proposes a series of round-table discussions bringing together physicists, engineers, philosophers, science communicators, policy experts, and industry representatives. These sessions will provide participants with the opportunity to explore the ethical and social aspects of scientific progress while engaging directly with leading voices from academia, industry, and public institutions.</p>
      <p>Designed as highly interactive events, the round-tables will encourage audience participation and open discussion, fostering a dialogue that extends beyond disciplinary boundaries.</p>
      <p>Find below some of the themes currently under consideration.</p>
      <p>The final selection will be refined throughout the conference preparation, taking into account the social and geopolitical state of the world in 2028, the availability of invited speakers, and the interests of the attendees.</p>

      <h3>Nuclear energy in the XXI century</h3>
      <p>As the world seeks pathways toward decarbonization and energy security, nuclear technologies are once again at the centre of public debate.</p>
      <p>This round-table will address the opportunities and challenges associated with contemporary nuclear energy. Some possible topics below.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Small Modular Reactors (SMRs),</li>
        <li>Advanced fission technologies,</li>
        <li>Nuclear fusion research,</li>
        <li>Nuclear safety and waste management,</li>
        <li>Non-proliferation and disarmament,</li>
        <li>The role of nuclear power in a sustainable energy transition.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>By bringing together physicists, engineers, policymakers, and science communicators, the discussion aims to provide a balanced perspective on one of the most consequential technological questions of our time.</p>
      <p>Potential speakers include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>representatives from ITER,</li>
        <li>science communicator team of The Avvocato dell’Atomo,</li>
        <li>experts from ENEA (Italian National Agency for New Technologies, Energy and Sustainable Economic Development) and INFN (National Institute of Nuclear Physics),</li>
        <li>scholars involved in nuclear disarmament initiatives.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Artificial Intelligence in academia and society</h3>
      <p>Artificial intelligence is rapidly transforming the way science is conducted, from data analysis and simulation to scientific discovery itself. At the same time, its widespread adoption raises important questions regarding transparency, intellectual ownership, and the evolving relationship between humans and increasingly sophisticated computational tools.</p>
      <p>This discussion will explore the role of AI in scientific research, higher education, and society at large, bringing together experts in artificial intelligence, computational science, philosophers, and scientific methodologies.</p>
      <p>Potential speakers may include</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>researchers in high-performance computing, and physics, like the TeRABIT (Network for Research and Academic Big Data in ITaly),</p>
          <Figure src="speakers/fosca-giannotti.png" alt="Portrait of Fosca Giannotti" layout="inset" imageFirst={false}>
            <p>computer scientists such as <strong>Fosca Giannotti</strong> (Scuola Normale Superiore),</p>
          </Figure>
        </li>
        <li>representatives from the FAIR foundation (Future Artificial Intelligence Research),</li>
        <li>representatives from the scientific publishing sector,</li>
        <li>scholars specializing in the ethical and philosophical implications of emerging technologies, such as Ivana Bartoletti (Digital Ethics Center at Oxford University) or Adriano Fabris (Digital Ethics at the University of Pisa)</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <Figure src="speakers/ivana-bartoletti.png" alt="Portrait of Ivana Bartoletti" caption="Ivana Bartoletti" layout="full" />
        <Figure src="speakers/adriano-fabris.png" alt="Portrait of Adriano Fabris" caption="Adriano Fabris" layout="full" />
      </div>

      <h3>Peer review and scientific integrity</h3>
      <p>Scientific progress relies not only on discovery but also on verification.  In an era characterized by rapid information exchange and misinformation, understanding how scientific knowledge is validated has become more important than ever.</p>
      <p>Organized in collaboration with representatives from CICAP (Italian Committee for the Investigation of Claims of the Pseudosciences)  and members of the scientific community, this roundtable will discuss peer review, scientific skepticism, and ethics of science communication.</p>
      <Figure src="speakers/bobbybroccoli.png" alt="Portrait of BobbyBroccoli" layout="inset">
        <p>As part of the discussion, the Organizing Committee will explore the possibility of hosting a remote interview with <strong>BobbyBroccoli</strong>, a popular science communicator known for his in-depth investigations of scientific controversies, research misconduct, and the sociology of science. Such a contribution would provide an engaging perspective on the importance of transparency, integrity, and critical thinking in modern research.</p>
      </Figure>

      <h3>Space frontiers</h3>
      <p>From lunar exploration and commercial spaceflight to planetary defence, humanity is entering a new era of space exploration.</p>
      <p>This roundtable will examine the scientific, technological, and societal challenges associated with humanity's expansion beyond Earth, discussing both current achievements and future ambitions.</p>
      <p>Potential speakers include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <Figure src="speakers/roberto-battiston.png" alt="Portrait of Roberto Battiston" layout="inset">
